09:46.430 --> 09:51.310
So we start with the table scans, then the cluster scan and the nested loops.

09:51.310 --> 09:56.480
So we have several nested loops and then the aggregation and the final step so that now we have like

09:56.520 --> 10:01.080
a nice explanation of what this SQL is doing behind the scenes for my query.

10:01.120 --> 10:04.520
And you don't have to be an expert understanding the execution plan.

10:04.560 --> 10:06.360
You can ask the AI about it.

10:06.360 --> 10:11.720
Now what is very important is to understand where are the bottlenecks, what are the problems.

10:11.720 --> 10:13.360
So let's see what we have here.

10:13.360 --> 10:16.800
So let's say the first one we have a table scan which is really bad.

10:16.800 --> 10:21.040
That means this table the orders archive does not has any index.

10:21.040 --> 10:26.800
So it says table scan indicates a lack of useful index on the table which forces the engine to scan

10:26.880 --> 10:28.440
the whole table or rows.

10:28.480 --> 10:32.720
And now what is very important is the nested loops in the joins.

10:32.720 --> 10:35.400
This is really bad if you have big tables.

10:35.400 --> 10:38.520
So he's saying it's fine if you have like small data sets.

10:38.560 --> 10:42.120
But it's going to be really problematic if you have many rows.

10:42.120 --> 10:47.520
So as you can see, we are getting more knowledge about the issues that we have from our execution plan.

10:47.520 --> 10:50.320
And the last step it is the suggestions.

10:50.320 --> 10:54.360
So the first one and the most obvious one is to add an index to the orders.

10:54.360 --> 10:56.290
Archive the non-clustered index.

10:56.290 --> 11:00.930
Well, if there is no index at all, I would go first with a clustered index, not immediately with

11:00.930 --> 11:04.250
a non-clustered index and then some other best practices.

11:04.250 --> 11:08.410
But I think this one is very relevant is to change the join type.

11:08.410 --> 11:12.850
So you can use the hints in order to use a merge join or a hash join.

11:12.850 --> 11:14.570
So now we understand how it works.

11:14.570 --> 11:17.490
Where are the issues and what are the suggestions to fix it.

11:17.490 --> 11:17.850
